<BlogLayout
  title="Dental Implants vs. Bridges: Which Is Right for You?"
  description="Comparing the two most popular tooth replacement options for Fallbrook patients — cost, longevity, and what to expect from each procedure."
  date="2026-04-15"
  category="Restorative Dentistry"
>
  <p>Missing a tooth? You're not alone — millions of Americans are missing at least one tooth. The good news is modern dentistry offers excellent solutions. The two most common are <strong>dental implants</strong> and <strong>dental bridges</strong>. But which one is right for you? Let's break it down.</p>

  <h2>What Is a Dental Implant?</h2>
  <p>A dental implant is a titanium post surgically placed into your jawbone, topped with a custom crown that looks and functions exactly like a natural tooth. It replaces both the root and the visible portion of the missing tooth.</p>

  <h2>What Is a Dental Bridge?</h2>
  <p>A bridge uses the two healthy teeth on either side of the gap as anchors for a false tooth (pontic) that spans the space. The anchor teeth are prepared (shaved down) to support crowns that hold the bridge in place.</p>

  <h2>Head-to-Head Comparison</h2>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Dental Implant</th><th>Dental Bridge</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Longevity</strong></td><td>Lifetime (with proper care)</td><td>10–15 years</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Impact on adjacent teeth</strong></td><td>None — stands alone</td><td>Requires grinding down healthy teeth</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Jawbone preservation</strong></td><td>Yes — prevents bone loss</td><td>No — bone loss continues</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Appearance</strong></td><td>Most natural</td><td>Very good</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Procedure timeline</strong></td><td>3–6 months</td><td>2–3 visits over a few weeks</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Initial cost</strong></td><td>Higher</td><td>Lower</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Long-term value</strong></td><td>Excellent (lasts lifetime)</td><td>Good (needs replacement)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Which Should You Choose?</h2>
  <p><strong>Choose an implant if:</strong> you want the most durable, natural-feeling solution, you have sufficient jawbone density, and you're willing to invest in a long-term solution.</p>
  <p><strong>Choose a bridge if:</strong> you want a faster, lower upfront cost solution, the adjacent teeth already need crowns, or you're not a candidate for implant surgery.</p>

